"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 11 and an ISUP grade group 4 finding on the prostatectomy specimen. Positive findings include negative surgical margins and no seminal vesicle invasion. Countervailing points are the absence of nodal sampling, which widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. Furthermore, there is a discrepancy between the biopsy result (Gleason 3+3) and the prostatectomy pathology (Gleason 4+3). The lack of documented postoperative PSA trajectory limits prognostic certainty. Information regarding subsequent PSA levels would be crucial for refining this assessment. Given the current data, the prognosis remains subject to the unknown nodal status and the discrepancy between biopsy and surgical findings. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 40.81–84.77 months; its coverage has not been validated."
